1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,569
A 200-year-old camera trick makes every chip in your phone.

2
00:00:03,916 --> 00:00:08,145
Around 1827, Nicéphore Niépce put a thin layer of asphalt on a

3
00:00:08,245 --> 00:00:12,264
metal plate. He placed the plate in a camera at his window.

4
00:00:12,600 --> 00:00:15,808
Light made the asphalt hard. Lavender oil removed the

5
00:00:15,908 --> 00:00:18,430
soft parts. The hard parts made a picture.

6
00:00:18,766 --> 00:00:20,885
That is the trick: coat, light, wash.

7
00:00:21,233 --> 00:00:24,278
In 1957, two scientists at a U.S. Army lab

8
00:00:24,378 --> 00:00:29,145
needed tiny transistors. Jay Lathrop turned his microscope upside

9
00:00:29,245 --> 00:00:32,141
down, so the lens made patterns smaller.

10
00:00:32,483 --> 00:00:35,542
They used the same trick, with a coating from Kodak.

11
00:00:35,642 --> 00:00:39,188
We still call it photolithography: light, stone and writing.

12
00:00:39,533 --> 00:00:44,280
Then chips helped cameras in return. In 1975, a Kodak engineer built

13
00:00:44,380 --> 00:00:48,628
the first digital camera you could carry. Its eye was a chip.

14
00:00:48,966 --> 00:00:51,800
Today, the machines that print chips are giant

15
00:00:51,900 --> 00:00:54,861
cameras. Nikon, Canon and Zeiss help build them.

16
00:00:55,200 --> 00:00:57,903
Cameras taught us how to make chips. Then chips

17
00:00:58,003 --> 00:01:00,646
gave cameras new eyes. Same trick, two worlds.

